00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.760 align:start position:0%
since its emergence in Wuhan China the  corona virus pandemic has tightened its  grip on the world six months ago the  w-h-o announced that the corona virus  could spread between people now is  reached almost every country in the  world shutting down economies and  forcing whole continents to enact  emergency responses the most rapid  spread of the virus is now in South  America to date there have been 14  million infections and 600,000 deaths  and this number is rising every day the  global scientific machine has jumped  into action and scientists have learned  a lot about the virus but there are  still many questions researchers are  working to answer why do people respond  so differently to infection  different people have strikingly  different responses to cope it 19  infection some never develop symptoms  whereas other seemingly healthy people  have severe and sometimes fatal  pneumonias there are multiple teams of  researchers investigating the  possibility that there could be a  genetic component the first strong  genetic links to severe kovat 19  suggested the people who developed  respiratory failure are more likely to  carry either one of two gene variants  these variations only seem to play a  small part in the disease but it's hoped  that further research will uncover more  significant genetic markers for severe  symptoms many studies have also shown  striking variations between social and  ethnic groups the causes of these  disparities are not clear but it is  likely that they're linked to  socio-economic inequality can we develop  immunity and if so how long does it last  this is a big question and immunologists  are working feverishly to determine what  immunity to SARS cough to could  studies have found that the immune  system can create high levels of  neutralizing antibodies against SARS  cough - which remain high for a few  weeks after infection but then typically  begin to wane  antibodies might linger higher levels  for longer when people have suffered a  more severe infection but researchers  still don't know the level of  neutralizing antibodies needed to  prevent reinfection or reduce kovat 19  symptoms ultimately antibodies are just  one part of the immunity story other  immune cells called t-cells are  important for long-term immunity -  researchers must piece together this  patchwork of immune responses and  compare them with infections from other  viruses to estimate how durable  protection might be  as the virus developed any worrying  mutations all viruses mutate as they  infect people and SARS cough - is no  exception  molecular epidemiologists have used  these mutations to trace the global  spread of the virus but scientists are  also on the lookout for changes that  affect the properties of the virus for  instance making it more or less virulent  or transmissible identifying worrying  mutations is tricky as most changes  won't affect how the virus spreads in  humans in fact these mutations won't  become apparent until the virus needs to  mutate to survive and that won't happen  until most of the world's population has  been infected or developed immunity how  well will a vaccine work an effective  vaccine may be the only way out of the  pandemic there are currently about 200  candidates in development worldwide with  around a dozen in clinical trials  several of which have already reported  that their vaccines seem safe the first  large-scale efficacy trials are set to  begin this summer but data from early  stage human trials and animal studies  seem tentatively hopeful studies in  macaques suggests that vaccines may do a  good job preventing lung infection but  not elsewhere in the body such as the  nose results such as this raised the  possibility of a COBIT 19 vaccine that  prevents severe disease but not the  spread of the virus what is the origin  of the virus most researchers now agree  that SARS cough - originated in  horseshoe bats but it's not yet known if  it jumped directly from bats to humans  rat g30 a coronavirus found in bats is  96% identical to SARS cough - but that  4% difference  represents decades of evolution possibly  in an intermediary species according to  some researchers this could be Malayan  pangolins but to unequivocally trace the  virus's journey to people scientists  would need to find an almost identical  version in an animal a prospect that is  further complicated by the fact that the  virus has been shown to spread back from  people to animals with cats dogs and  farmed minks all showing signs of  infection more than six months after the  emergence of kovat 19 scientists  continue to search for answers and while  progress has been made it's clear there  is still a long road ahead  [Music]
 

